/-!
# A counterexample behaviour for THE statement at g = 1, κ = 18/25 (shard C)

**Problem, plainly.** Is the sequential statement `NetCorrectSeq` true for THE target (gcd_ps_e6bb4aec) when
every arc may take any delay in its fast-corner window widened by κ = 18/25 (`Adm pK 0`), with no runt filtering?

**Proved (if this file checks).** `xK` (generated by formal/tools/huff/simadv.py --exact, gen_cex.py; 89,170
transitions) is a behaviour of `circuit DecodedPS.net DecodedPS.boundary` at `pK = ⟨1, 18/25⟩`, corner 0
(`behaves_k`: every gate kernel-checked at every breakpoint): the input-ack C-element `umux16.uya`, the four storage nodes of its support (ulink_n_falseResult_85 and
ulink_n_trueResult_72 stage 0, umux16.uj1, SLICE_X32Y28.A6) and the pips of their input routes read at ⌊43·FAST_MAX/25⌋, every other pin at ⌈7·FAST_MIN/25⌉. The environment offers one pair
(1071, 462) with reset 400 ns, data constant, the shared request raised at 401 ns and lowered once all three input
acknowledges are high, pend's acknowledge following 3 ns later; it satisfies `Env` (any `su`, `Δo ≤ 3000`) and
`SeqEnv`; every arbiter satisfies `ArbOK T` for `T ≥ 62`. But out0's request never rises: the circuit has
deadlocked, so `Correct` fails (`k_not_correct`). Hence `not_netCorrectSeq`: for every `T ≥ 62`, `su` and
`Δo ≤ 3000`, `¬ NetCorrectSeq net boundary arbs pK T 400000 su Δo`.

**Assumptions used.** Only Spec's own definitions (H_DECODE: `DecodedPS.net` is the bitstream; H_PRIM/H_DELAY as
`Behaves`), standard axioms. **Scope.** No runt filtering (w = 0): the run has sub-20 ps pulses; under
`SpecRunt.NetCorrectSeqRunt 20` this is not a counterexample. κ = 18/25 is a very large scatter.
-/

